[[local:zz.texts/russell1905-onDenoting.pdf]]!!!

= main =
According to the Stanford Encyclopedia entry [[https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/win2025/entries/meaning/|_Theories of Meaning_]], Russellian
semantics is the doctrine that the constituents of propositions (i.e. sentence
meanings) are “objects, properties, relations and functions”.

Russellian semantics is basically [[fregean_semantics]] (Frege's distinction
between sense and reference) with the added claim that senses (at least the
fundamental ones) are composed of the objects, properties (and relationships) of
the objects that they represent. (Though in these cases the distinction between
sense and reference may collapse.)

According to Russell:
    when there is an anything with which we do not have immediate acquaintance,
    but only definition by denoting phrases, then the propositions in which this
    thing is introduced by means of a denoting phrase do not really contain this
    thing as a constituent, but contain instead the constituents expressed by
    the several words of the denoting phrase. Thus in every proposition that we
    can apprehend (i.e. not only in those whose truth or falsehood we can judge
    of, but in all that we can think about), all the constituents are really
    entities with which we have immediate acquaintance. <br>
    [[local:zz.texts/russell1905-onDenoting.pdf]]

The objects and properties that are directly *named* by genuine names and
predicates are sensibilia (sense data - but they can exist independently of any
mind) and their properties. (These sensibilia are the basic elements of
Russell's metaphysics and ordinary physical objects are “logical constructions”
from them). But note that we do not have any names for individual sensibilia and
hence it seems there are no genuine names at all. Russell's account of these
semantic foundations works best when applied to predicates. We are directly
acquainted with the property red, and it is impossible for us to form the
judgement that we are seeing a red sensible without there being a red sensible.
Our having the thought guarantees the existence of the property.

Still note the similarity to the traditional Aristotelian account of
representation – at least within the fundamental sphere to which we
have direct epistemic (and semantic) access.
(see
[[wn.RLN:collapse_of_traditional_European_accounts_of_reasoning# second problem: the content of scientific theories]]
)

== Russellian-Millian semantics ==
The Millian addition is that the meaning of a “simple proper name” is the
object it refers to. Odd since, at least on its most obvious reading Russell's
account does away with simple proper names altogether (unless one takes
predicates to be names of properties, but even then predicates are not simple
proper names).

== descriptivism ==
[[local:zz.texts/russell1905-onDenoting.pdf]]
It is odd that the chief objection to “Russellian-Millian” semantics raised in
the Stanford Encyclopedia entry is the problem of informative
identities, since Russell's most celebrated article (largely responsible for
establishing his early reputation as an first rate philosopher) was devoted to
proposing a solution.

=== problems ===
==== informative identities ====
If the meaning of a name is the object it denotes (i.e. two names are
synonymous if they denote the same object) then the principle that substituting
one synonym for another preserves the meaning of the overall sentence requires
that the following two sentences are synonyms:
    1)    Clark Kent is Clark Kent.
    2)    Clark Kent is Superman.
So too the following two sentences should be synonymous, but they differ in
meaning so significantly that the first is true and the second false:
    3)    Lois believes that Clark Kent is Clark Kent.
    4)    Lois believes that Clark Kent is Superman.
The usual reason given for believing that (1) and (2) differ in meaning is their
means of justification.  (1) is justified by the _a priori_ insight that each
thing is identical to itself, (2) by _a posteriori_ investigation. The
difference in truth value between (3) and (4) is enough to establish that they
are different in meaning (and hence that the sentential clause versions of (1)
and (2) that they contain must differ in meaning at least in this context).
(see [[truth_conditional_semantics#objections#propositional attitudes]])

==== meaningful negative existentials ====
If the meaning of a name is the object it denotes, then what about the meaning
of denials of existence such as “Prester John does not exist”. If Prester John
does not exist then how can a meaningful sentence contain a meaningless name?
But if Prester John does exist, then the denial is false. So no denial of
existence can ever be true! (A problem raised as far back as Parmenides' poem
_On Nature_.) Nor indeed any meaningful statements about non-existent objects,
such as “The King of France is bald.”

What then are the meanings of names if not the object they denote?

=== solution ===
Russell's solution to this “Millian problem” was to claim that “Superman” (at
least, but “Clark Kent” also) is not a “simple proper name” but a (hidden)
definite description. The name “Superman” is synonymous with a definite
description (say “the one and only humanoid born on Krypton possessing
superhuman strength, the power of flight, superhuman vision and hearing, spends
much of his time of Earth, often wears red and blue” or some more extended
version including all the information about Superman that is provided to us in
the canonical media).

Thus (2) is synonymous with:
    2')   Clark Kent is the one and only humanoid born on Krypton possessing superhuman strength ...
Now “Clark Kent” is presumably also a definite description. Perhaps something
like “the one and only tall male who works as a reporter at the Daily Planet,
wears glasses etc.,”. In any case (1) with or without “Clark Kent” replaced by
its synonymous definite description is clearly different in meaning from (2').

So too it may well be true that:
    3') Lois believes that the one and only tall male who works as a reporter at
        the Daily Planet and wears glasses is the one and only tall male who
        works as a reporter ...
and yet false that:
    4') Lois believes that the one and only tall male who works as a reporter
        at the Daily Planet and wear glasses is the one and only humanoid born
        on Krypton ...

It is also straightforwardly false that:
    One and only one person is King of France and that person is bald.

Hence the problem posed by the informativeness of some identity statements and
the meaningfulness of negative existentials is resolved if these names are
really definite descriptions.

=== denoting phrases meaningless in isolation ===
Above Russell's solution was described as hinging on the claim that (some) names
_are_ hidden definite descriptions. Which is to say that (some) names
_are synonymous with_ definite descriptions. (e.g. that “Superman” is synonymous
with “the one and only humanoid from Krypton ...”). Russell himself frequently
describes his suggestion in this way:

    The names that we commonly use, like “Socrates”, are really abbreviations
    for descriptions...
				[[local:zz.texts/russell1918-thePhilosophyOfLogicalAtomism.pdf]]
	
    “Apollo” means really “the object having such and such properties”. say “the
    object having the properties enumerated in the Classical Dictionary”
														 _Principia Mathematica_

But in _On Denoting_ Russell instead claims that denoting phrases (including
definite descriptions) have no meaning at all.
    According to the view which I advocate, a denoting phrase is essentially
    part of a sentence, and does not, like most single words, have any
    significance on its own account.

WHY? To accept that “the meanings of words are abstractions from the truth
conditions of the sentences that contain them”((_Five milestones of empiricism_ in [[local:zz.texts/quine1981-theoriesAndThings.pdf]])) is not to deny that they have
meanings at all. If substituting one phrase for another always produces a
sentence synonymous with the original, then we may well say that the two phrases
are themselves synonymous. And if “Superman” is synonymous (in this sense) with
“the one and only humanoid born on Krypton ...”  (as well as a range of
other phrases) we can (if there are no better candidates) identify this class of
synonyms as their common meaning.

Indeed, even in the preceding quote Russell explicitly states that “most single
words” have “significance on [their] own account”. So Russell is not claiming
that sentences are the only bearers of meaning and none of the constituent parts
have meaning by themselves. What then is the reason for claiming that these
denoting phrases have no meaning in isolation?
    The phrase per se has no meaning, because in any proposition in which it
    occurs the proposition, fully expressed, does not contain the phrase, which
    has been broken up.
This is implausible. Russell (as well as nearly everyone else) thinks that
statements have meanings (i.e. propositions). Statements/propositions are
complex “logical constructions” from meaningful basic elements (predicates which
express directly known properties). Like predicates(/properties) that have
meaning in isolation definite descriptions contribute to the meaning of the
statements(/propositions) that contain them.  So there appears to be no reason
not to admit definite descriptions as meaningful, though admittedly incomplete,
logical constructions from the same materials.

Certainly a contributing factor is that Russell sometimes says that the meaning
of a name is the object it refers to:
    A name, in the narrow logical sense of a word whose meaning is a particular,
    can only be applied to a particular with which the speaker is acquainted
					[[local:zz.texts/russell1918-thePhilosophyOfLogicalAtomism.pdf]]
There is however, a powerful ulterior motive lurking nearby. Russell's
self-proclaimed objective in _Principia Mathematica_ was to reduce mathematics
to (Fregean first order) logic. In this the number one was identified with the
set of all the sets that contain one object. (The set of all the sets S such
that ∃x∀y x∊S ∧.y∊S ⊃ y=x.) This set was then identified with the propositional
function “... is a set such that ∃x∀y x∊S ∧.y∊S ⊃ y=x”. And then that
propositional function (the complex predicate involved in a definite
description) could be dispensed with as a mere “incomplete symbol”. Leaving us
with a mathematics that carries no ontological commitments at all, and hence the
reduction to first order logic (which is supposed to carry no ontological
commitments) was complete.
(this ulterior motive is confirmed in [[local:zz.texts/soames2007-noClassRussellOnContextualDefinitionAndTheEliminationOfSets.pdf]])
(see also [[carnaps_quasi-analysis]])

A third and more immediate motivation, was that it allowed Russell to avoid the
objection that “The author of Waverley ...” is not felt to be synonymous with
“∃y y wrote Waverley and ∀x(x wrote Waverley ⊃ x=y) and ... (y)”. Instead it
allows us to compare the truth conditions of the 'analysed' statement (in which
the definite description no longer occurs) with the original and we can 'see'
that the two have the same truth conditions and are hence synonymous (as per
[[truth_conditional_semantics]] and [[fregean_semantics#Freges criterion of synonymy]]).

==== contextual definition ====
[[contextual_definition]]
Despite the above misgivings about Russell's claim that definite descriptions
“never have any meaning in isolation”
provide synonyms for names, there is still a sense in which contextual
definition plays a pivotal role in the history of (analytic) philosophy.

Neither of these is a good reason to deny that either propositional
functions or definite descriptions have meaning.

===== eliminative definition =====
[[Leibniz#antecedent of eliminative definition]]+
Despite this, contextual definition remains a pivotal technique in logic and
philosophy. It emerges as eliminative definition: an unimpeachable way to
specify or guarantee the meaningfulness of some term or phrase. If we can show
that 


as Quine puts it
    the only justification of our introducing such notations [by definition],
    unofficially as it were, is the assurance of their unique eliminability in
    favor of primitive notation. 
Definition is “legitimation by elimination”(([[local:zz.texts/quine1969-ontologicalRelativityAOE.pdf]]))

Eliminative definition

[[Quinean_semantics]] to meaning

=== objections to descriptivism ===
See [[evans_theory_of_names#Kripkes objections]]

== objections to other aspects of Russell's semantics ==
I seem to remember an issue along the lines: if true propositions are facts then
what are the false propositions? If the proposition that a statement expresses
is “objects properties and relations” in a particular
structure/arrangement/state of affairs then what about false statements. If
there is no such arrangement then is the statement meaningless? If there is then
is the statement true?

This is one of the “museum theories” of language to which Quine so
opposed. The indeterminacy of translation is the key objection to sentence
meanings.
